//! THE REFERENCE COUNT, AND WHAT ZERO DOES (sawos design 7 D-1/D-2) — the
//! per-kind matrix in one place, on `waitables`' own model.
//!
//! Every countable slab slot carries a `refs`, and everything that can be said
//! about maintaining it is one of three questions, each answered by an
//! EXHAUSTIVE match on `ObjType`:
//!
//!   `ref_object`     something now names this object
//!   `unref_object`   something no longer does — and zero FREES, right here
//!   `free_object`    the slot goes back, and the kind's last rites run
//!
//! That is a MATRIX rather than a funnel because the answers genuinely differ
//! per kind — an Interrupt's last rites mask a device line, a Timer's
//! reprogram the comparator, a Waiter's detach a list, a Mapping's deliberately
//! leave a protection row installed — and it is written as three functions in
//! one place so the matrix is READABLE AS ONE. A kind added to `ObjType` fails
//! all three to compile until somebody says what references it, which is the
//! property a `_` arm anywhere here would destroy.
//!
//! # ZERO FREES, SYNCHRONOUSLY (D-1)
//!
//! The kernel is one context running to completion, so the count hits zero
//! INSIDE the very syscall that dropped the last reference and the free is a
//! plain call: no deferred reclamation, no cleanup queue, nothing to drain at a
//! later point. Cascades compose out of the same two functions — releasing a
//! Waiter to zero detaches its attachments, which may drop an Event to zero,
//! which frees and credits — and they terminate because an attachment is
//! counted on the WAITABLE and never on the Waiter, so the graph is a forest.
//!
//! WHAT ZERO DOES NOT FREE IS A RANGE. A freed Memory slot returns to the slab
//! and its bytes return to no pool: the front-cut parent cannot absorb an
//! arbitrary hole (the one-`{base,len}` shape, design 6 D-2). Quotas count
//! OBJECTS, not bytes, in v1; byte accounting arrives with a real allocator,
//! and §2.5's pages-free-on-last-reference clause is narrowed accordingly in
//! the spec.
//!
//! # WHY THIS IS A MODULE AND WHERE IT SITS
//!
//! Between `process` and `wake`, and the call graph is what puts it there. It
//! has to reach EVERY slab — the two memory slabs and the handle table below in
//! `objects`, the Timer slab in `time`, the three wait slabs in `waitables` —
//! and also `PROCESSES`, because a Process's own count is what design 3 D-3's
//! reclaim now reads. Written any lower it could not see the process table;
//! written any higher it would be above the teardown that also frees these
//! slabs. It reaches nothing above itself.
//!
//! Two mints are NOT expressed through this matrix and could not be: the boot
//! path's, in `process`, which sits below this file. They cost nothing to write
//! out — a boot Memory and IoMemory are created with `refs: 1` in the slot
//! literal (a fresh object's first reference is not a change to a count, it IS
//! the count) and root's own Process handle is one increment on its own table.
//! Every GENERIC site — where a handle is bound to a kind the code does not
//! know — comes through here.

import hal
import sosabi.{EventMode, ObjType}
import kcore.diag.{fatal_kernel}
import kcore.objects.{
    IOMEMORIES,
    IoMemorySlot,
    MAPPINGS,
    MEMORIES,
    MappingSlot,
    MemorySlot,
    NO_ROW,
    QuotaKind,
    SlotState,
    quota_credit,
}
import kcore.time.{TIMERS, TimerSlot, reprogram_timer}
import kcore.waitables.{
    ATTACHMENTS,
    Attachment,
    EVENTS,
    EventSlot,
    INTERRUPTS,
    InterruptSlot,
    WAITERS,
    WaitableKind,
    WaiterSlot,
    set_waitable_attachment,
}
import kcore.process.{PROCESSES, reclaim_process_slot}

/// Something now names object `slot` of kind `kind`.
///
/// Called BESIDE every generic bind: `mint_handle` at each op that hands out a
/// handle onto an object that already existed, `give`'s child-side bind, and
/// `waiter_add`'s attachment (through `ref_waitable`). A creation site does not
/// call it — a fresh slot is written with `refs: 1`, because its first
/// reference is the count rather than a change to one.
///
/// FOUR KINDS ARE NOT COUNTED, and each arm says which reason it is:
///
///   - `System` has no slab at all. It is the singleton every process's boot
///     handle names, so there is no slot to return and nothing to return it to.
///   - `Clock` is EXEMPT — kernel-eternal, owned by nobody (the Aug-17 user
///     ruling). Freeing a domain's slot on a release would take the machine's
///     monotonic counter away from everybody else.
///   - `Thread` is NOT COUNTED IN v1: the join/exit protocol owns a thread
///     slot's lifetime and does it on terms a handle count cannot express
///     (see `ThreadSlot`). Recorded, deferred.
///   - `Unbound` names nothing, so a bind of it is not a thing that happens.
public(package) func ref_object(kind: ObjType, slot: Int) unsafe {
    match kind {
        case Event -> { EVENTS[slot].refs = EVENTS[slot].refs + 1 },
        case Waiter -> { WAITERS[slot].refs = WAITERS[slot].refs + 1 },
        case Interrupt -> { INTERRUPTS[slot].refs = INTERRUPTS[slot].refs + 1 },
        case Timer -> { TIMERS[slot].refs = TIMERS[slot].refs + 1 },
        case Memory -> { MEMORIES[slot].refs = MEMORIES[slot].refs + 1 },
        case IoMemory -> { IOMEMORIES[slot].refs = IOMEMORIES[slot].refs + 1 },
        case Mapping -> { MAPPINGS[slot].refs = MAPPINGS[slot].refs + 1 },
        case Process -> { PROCESSES[slot].refs = PROCESSES[slot].refs + 1 },
        case System -> {},
        case Clock -> {},
        case Thread -> {},
        case Unbound -> {},
    }
}

/// Take one reference away, and answer whether that was the last.
///
/// SPLIT OUT FROM `unref_object` FOR ONE CALLER — the teardown, which must
/// decrement without freeing. See `unref_teardown` for why that is not an
/// inconsistency.
///
/// The zero test before the subtraction is not defensive bookkeeping: a
/// teardown zeroes the slots a process owned, so a LATER decrement from another
/// process's close-all can arrive at a slot whose count is already gone, and
/// the honest answer for "take one away from nothing" is that nothing was taken
/// and nothing reached zero.
func drop_reference(kind: ObjType, slot: Int) unsafe -> Bool {
    match kind {
        case Event -> {
            if EVENTS[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            EVENTS[slot].refs = EVENTS[slot].refs - 1
            EVENTS[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case Waiter -> {
            if WAITERS[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            WAITERS[slot].refs = WAITERS[slot].refs - 1
            WAITERS[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case Interrupt -> {
            if INTERRUPTS[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            INTERRUPTS[slot].refs = INTERRUPTS[slot].refs - 1
            INTERRUPTS[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case Timer -> {
            if TIMERS[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            TIMERS[slot].refs = TIMERS[slot].refs - 1
            TIMERS[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case Memory -> {
            if MEMORIES[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            MEMORIES[slot].refs = MEMORIES[slot].refs - 1
            MEMORIES[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case IoMemory -> {
            if IOMEMORIES[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            IOMEMORIES[slot].refs = IOMEMORIES[slot].refs - 1
            IOMEMORIES[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case Mapping -> {
            if MAPPINGS[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            MAPPINGS[slot].refs = MAPPINGS[slot].refs - 1
            MAPPINGS[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case Process -> {
            if PROCESSES[slot].refs == 0 { return false }
            PROCESSES[slot].refs = PROCESSES[slot].refs - 1
            PROCESSES[slot].refs == 0
        },
        case System -> false,
        case Clock -> false,
        case Thread -> false,
        case Unbound -> false,
    }
}

/// Something no longer names object `slot` of kind `kind`. **ZERO FREES.**
///
/// The ordinary decrement, and the one every op uses: `release`, `give`'s
/// caller-side unbind, `Waiter.Remove`'s detach, and the cascade out of a freed
/// Waiter. It is a plain call because the kernel runs to completion — the free
/// happens inside the syscall that dropped the last reference, so there is no
/// window in which a zero-reference object is reachable.
public(package) func unref_object(kind: ObjType, slot: Int) unsafe {
    if drop_reference(kind, slot) {
        free_object(kind, slot)
    }
}

/// The teardown's decrement: it counts, and it DOES NOT FREE.
///
/// **THE ASYMMETRY IS THE POINT, and it is what keeps design 7 D-5's authorized
/// set the size D-5 says it is.** A teardown frees what a process OWNED, by
/// ownership, in per-slab sweeps that also COUNT what they freed for the
/// report; if the close-all above those sweeps freed by reference instead,
/// every one of those counts would collapse and every teardown line in the
/// suite would move — for objects that were being reclaimed either way, in the
/// same function, microseconds apart. So the close-all keeps the ledger honest
/// (a handle in a dying table really is one reference fewer, and objects
/// OTHER processes own must see that) and the sweeps below it do the freeing
/// they have always done.
///
/// For the dying process's OWN objects the two agree by construction: the sweep
/// zeroes the slot, count included, so nothing can be left half-counted.
public(package) func unref_teardown(kind: ObjType, slot: Int) unsafe {
    let _ = drop_reference(kind, slot)
}

/// Give object `slot` of kind `kind` back to its slab, and run the kind's own
/// last rites.
///
/// **THE LAST RITES ARE WHAT MAKE THIS A MATRIX AND NOT A LOOP.** Three kinds
/// have something to say to the machine or to the rest of the kernel on the way
/// out, and the reasons are the teardown's own, arriving one object at a time
/// instead of one process at a time:
///
///   - an INTERRUPT MASKS ITS LINE. A device the dropping process was driving
///     may still be asserting, and an unmasked line with no object behind it
///     would be taken, reported and masked once per fire forever. Masking is
///     the kernel taking the line back — §9's handover run backwards — and it
///     is what makes the line BINDABLE AGAIN, mid-life, which no release could
///     achieve before this unit.
///   - a TIMER REPROGRAMS THE COMPARATOR. A dropped armed deadline is one the
///     kernel would otherwise keep waking itself for with nobody left to tell.
///   - a WAITER DETACHES ITS LIST, which is the one cascade in the system.
///
/// And one kind has something deliberate NOT to do — see the `Mapping` arm.
///
/// THE QUOTA IS CREDITED AGAINST THE SLOT'S OWN `process` FIELD, which is
/// creator-pays (D-3): whoever was charged for the object is who gets it back,
/// whichever process happened to drop the last handle. `Mapping` and `Process`
/// are the two arms with no credit here, each for its own stated reason.
public(package) func free_object(kind: ObjType, slot: Int) unsafe {
    match kind {
        case Event -> {
            // AN ATTACHED EVENT CANNOT BE HERE — an attachment is a counted
            // reference (D-1's table), so reaching zero means nothing is
            // watching. The test is the invariant written down: if it ever
            // fired, the count and the wait machinery would have disagreed.
            if EVENTS[slot].attachment != 0 {
                fatal_kernel("an event reached zero references while attached",
                             slot as UInt)
            }
            let owner = EVENTS[slot].process
            EVENTS[slot] = EventSlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0,
                                     mode: EventMode.Or, word: 0,
                                     attachment: 0, refs: 0)
            quota_credit(owner, QuotaKind.Event)
        },
        case Waiter -> {
            // **THE ONE CASCADE.** A Waiter's attachments are references it
            // HOLDS, so its death releases them — and each release may take a
            // waitable to zero, which frees and credits through this same
            // function. The list is walked whole rather than through
            // `detach_attachment`, because there is no list left to unlink
            // from: the Waiter is going.
            //
            // A THREAD STILL PARKED ON THIS WAITER IS STRANDED, and that is the
            // release ruling's "legal-but-doomed" seen from one process's own
            // inside: releasing the last handle to the Waiter a sibling thread
            // is parked on is the same class of self-inflicted mistake as
            // releasing your System handle. Nothing is corrupted — the parked
            // thread simply never wakes, and the deadlock report is what names
            // it.
            var cur = WAITERS[slot].attached
            while cur != 0 {
                let a = cur - 1
                let next = ATTACHMENTS[a].link
                let watched = ATTACHMENTS[a].kind
                let target = ATTACHMENTS[a].target
                ATTACHMENTS[a] = Attachment(state: SlotState.Free,
                                            kind: WaitableKind.Event, target: 0,
                                            waiter: 0, key: 0, link: 0)
                set_waitable_attachment(watched, target, 0)
                unref_waitable(watched, target)
                cur = next
            }
            let owner = WAITERS[slot].process
            WAITERS[slot] = WaiterSlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0,
                                       attached: 0, blocked: 0, refs: 0)
            quota_credit(owner, QuotaKind.Waiter)
        },
        case Interrupt -> {
            if INTERRUPTS[slot].attachment != 0 {
                fatal_kernel("an interrupt reached zero references while attached",
                             slot as UInt)
            }
            let owner = INTERRUPTS[slot].process
            hal.irq_mask(INTERRUPTS[slot].line)
            INTERRUPTS[slot] = InterruptSlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0,
                                             line: 0, pending: false,
                                             attachment: 0, refs: 0)
            quota_credit(owner, QuotaKind.Interrupt)
        },
        case Timer -> {
            if TIMERS[slot].attachment != 0 {
                fatal_kernel("a timer reached zero references while attached",
                             slot as UInt)
            }
            let owner = TIMERS[slot].process
            TIMERS[slot] = TimerSlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0, clock: 0,
                                     armed: false, deadline_ns: 0,
                                     interval_ns: 0, fires: 0, attachment: 0,
                                     refs: 0)
            reprogram_timer()
            quota_credit(owner, QuotaKind.Timer)
        },
        case Memory -> {
            // THE SLOT COMES BACK; THE RANGE GOES NOWHERE. A front-cut parent
            // cannot absorb an arbitrary hole, so the bytes stay spent until
            // an allocator exists to spend them again (design 6 D-2; §2.5's
            // narrowing).
            let owner = MEMORIES[slot].process
            MEMORIES[slot] = MemorySlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0,
                                        base: 0, len: 0, refs: 0)
            quota_credit(owner, QuotaKind.Memory)
        },
        case IoMemory -> {
            // MMIO IS PINNED, so this touches no hardware whatever: the
            // registers were there before the window and will be there after
            // it. What actually revokes a driver's reach is the ROW, and a row
            // is a Mapping.
            let owner = IOMEMORIES[slot].process
            IOMEMORIES[slot] = IoMemorySlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0,
                                            base: 0, len: 0, refs: 0)
            quota_credit(owner, QuotaKind.IoMemory)
        },
        case Mapping -> {
            // **THE SLOT FREES AND THE ROW STAYS** (sawos design 7 D-2), and
            // this arm is §2.5's own sentence made mechanical rather than an
            // omission. A Mapping OWNS its row; the row does not own the
            // Mapping. So a mapped-but-unreferenced Mapping — every handle
            // released, the grant still installed — is exactly §2.5's
            // "dropped without unmap = permanent, safe-but-leaked", and the row
            // becomes unremovable until the TARGET's teardown, which is what
            // "leaked" has always meant. Removing it here would be a
            // revocation nobody asked for: the process whose domain carries it
            // is a different process from the one that dropped the handle.
            //
            // NO QUOTA IS CREDITED HERE, and that is the consequence of the row
            // being what the mapping quota counts (see `QuotaKind.Mapping`).
            // The charge follows the ROW: `Unmap` credits it, the target's
            // teardown zeroes it, and a leaked row stays charged for exactly as
            // long as it stays installed — which is what keeps `used` and
            // `grant_count` in step and `install_row`'s `fatal` unreachable.
            MAPPINGS[slot] = MappingSlot(state: SlotState.Free, process: 0,
                                         target: 0, row: NO_ROW, refs: 0)
        },
        case Process -> {
            // A PROCESS IS A WAITABLE SINCE M3 UNIT 5.5, so it joins the three
            // arms above in asserting the invariant an attachment-as-reference
            // buys (sawos design 8): reaching zero means nothing is watching,
            // because a watcher IS a reference. The test is the invariant
            // written down, and it is what makes attach-after-death sound
            // against the reclaim below — if it ever fired, a supervisor would
            // be parked on a slot the kernel had already given away.
            if PROCESSES[slot].attachment != 0 {
                fatal_kernel("a process reached zero references while attached",
                             slot as UInt)
            }
            // A LIVE PROCESS IS NOT FREED BY LOSING ITS LAST HANDLE — it is
            // still running, and its slot is what it is running in. Only a
            // `Gone` slot's last reader going makes the §8 status word
            // unreadable, and `reclaim_process_slot` is that rule, unchanged
            // from design 3 D-3 except that it now READS the count this unit
            // maintains instead of walking every table to recompute it.
            //
            // The ledger is not credited either: the teardown already zeroed
            // this slot's rows, and a process is not something another process
            // is charged for.
            reclaim_process_slot(slot)
        },
        case System -> {
            fatal_kernel("free_object on the System singleton", slot as UInt)
        },
        case Clock -> {
            fatal_kernel("free_object on a kernel-eternal Clock", slot as UInt)
        },
        case Thread -> {
            fatal_kernel("free_object on a Thread — the join protocol owns it",
                         slot as UInt)
        },
        case Unbound -> {
            fatal_kernel("free_object on a handle that names nothing",
                         slot as UInt)
        },
    }
}

/// The wait machinery's kinds, translated into the object kinds this matrix is
/// written over.
///
/// A SECOND ENUM MEETS THE FIRST HERE, and nowhere else. `WaitableKind` exists
/// because attaching also spends a right and which enum's bits that is depends
/// on the kind (`waitables`' header); `ObjType` is what a handle entry carries.
/// One translation written out beats a cast between two enums that happen to be
/// numbered differently, and it is total, so the third waitable kind cost it
/// one line — and so did the FOURTH (sawos design 8), which is where the two
/// enums genuinely stop being numbered alike: `WaitableKind.Process` is 3 and
/// `ObjType.Process` is not.
func waitable_obj_type(kind: WaitableKind) -> ObjType {
    match kind {
        case Event -> ObjType.Event,
        case Interrupt -> ObjType.Interrupt,
        case Timer -> ObjType.Timer,
        case Process -> ObjType.Process,
    }
}

/// An attachment now names this waitable (§2.2; sawos design 7 D-1).
public(package) func ref_waitable(kind: WaitableKind, slot: Int) unsafe {
    ref_object(waitable_obj_type(kind), slot)
}

/// An attachment no longer names this waitable. Zero frees, as everywhere.
public(package) func unref_waitable(kind: WaitableKind, slot: Int) unsafe {
    unref_object(waitable_obj_type(kind), slot)
}

/// The TEARDOWN's version: it counts, and it DOES NOT FREE — `unref_teardown`
/// reached through the wait machinery's own enum.
///
/// **IT EXISTS BECAUSE M3 UNIT 5.5 MADE AN ATTACHMENT ABLE TO CROSS PROCESSES**
/// (sawos design 8). Until a Process was waitable, every attachment joined a
/// Waiter and a waitable of the SAME process, so the teardown's attachment
/// sweep could free both ends and be done; a supervisor's attachment onto a
/// CHILD's Process slot is the first pair that spans two processes, and the
/// dying supervisor's side owes the other side a decrement exactly as the
/// close-all above it owes one for a handle onto somebody else's object. Same
/// asymmetry, same reason: the ledger stays honest and the per-slab sweeps keep
/// doing the freeing they have always done.
public(package) func unref_waitable_teardown(kind: WaitableKind, slot: Int) unsafe {
    unref_teardown(waitable_obj_type(kind), slot)
}
